=============================================================
  Cointegration Diagnostics
=============================================================

=== Entry gate: the factorisation contract ===

At r = 0 with diagonal Phi, Theta and Sigma the likelihood factorises,
so the joint fit must equal the sum of the univariate fits exactly.

  univariate 1 (p=2, q=0)   logL =     -24.1491940078
  univariate 2 (p=2, q=0)   logL =     -14.7874170649
                                 sum  =     -38.9366110727
                                 joint=     -38.9366110727
  (both sides untruncated; the fit's own logL, xi truncated at 1e-03, is -38.9366110727)

  crossing identity (joint - sum) = -2.274e-12   (tolerance 1.0e-06)   VERIFIED

  --- optimality certificate ---
  logL AT the values brought in   =     -42.7331411271
  logL of the diagonal fit        =     -38.9366110727
  optimality gap (fit - brought)  = +3.797e+00

  The gap is >= 0 always, and zero exactly when what came in were
  the univariate optima.  Here they were not: this input is A SPECIFICATION.


=============================================================
  Joint Hypothesis Tests (Wald)
=============================================================

  r = 0: no error-correction term, no Lambda and no B; the short-run block only

Short-run dynamics   [Gamma on nabla Y, Theta on the innovations]
  ! statements about nabla Y, not about the levels: a variable can drive
    another's DIFFERENCES and still be tied to it only through ec

Joint significance of the last short-run lag, Gamma(1):
  H0: Gamma(last) = 0
  Wald chi2(2) = 3.4863, p-value = 0.1750
  Cannot reject H0 -> the last AR lag is not significant.

No free cross effects to test (the short run is already diagonal or structured).

  ! p-values from the BFGS-accumulated covariance; use -fdhess before quoting them


=============================================================
  Impulse Response of the Levels
=============================================================

  Response of Y_{t+k} to a one-s.d. orthogonalized shock at t.
  Shocks are orthogonalized by Sigma = P D P', so the ORDERING is the
  .inp's column order: a different order gives different shocks.
  ! in a cointegrated system the response does not die out: what it
    converges to is the permanent effect, of rank M - r = 2.

